<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Silistra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durostorum, Drastar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Silistra is a town on the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, on the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>. As Roman Durostorum it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> base on the Danube frontier, and as Byzantine Dorostolon and Bulgarian Drastar it was an important fortress and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>'s seat. It was the scene of the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatoslav-i-of-kiev">Sviatoslav</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> in 971.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/silistra.jpg" alt="Silistra" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juju13620 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silistra_River_Danube.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Silistra lies on the southern bank of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the Bulgarian part of the historical region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobruja">Dobruja</a>, at the point where the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> stops following the river. It is the administrative centre of Silistra Province. The name is possibly derived from the root of Istrum, the old Thracian name of the lower Danube. Landmarks include a richly decorated Late Roman tomb, remains of the medieval fortress and an Ottoman fort.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moesia">Moesia</a> was founded in 12 CE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a fort in 29 on the site of an earlier Thracian settlement and kept its name, Durostorum. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajans-dacian-wars">Trajan's Dacian Wars</a> it was enlarged into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> fortress for the Legio XI Claudia, which stayed there from before 114 until about 400, and it grew into a city at the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-aurelius">Marcus Aurelius</a>.</p><p>Durostorum became a bishopric and a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the region. The earliest saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> are Roman soldiers executed there in the Diocletian Persecution, among them Saint Dasius, and the Roman general Flavius Aëtius was born in the town in 396.</p><p>Around the end of the 7th century the town was incorporated into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, as Drastar, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> was proclaimed the first patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. In 895 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simeon-i-of-bulgaria">Simeon I the Great</a> held the fortress against besieging Hungarians. Sviatoslav I of Kiev captured it in 969, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> took it two years later in the Battle of Dorostolon.</p><p>Tsar Samuel restored Bulgarian rule in 976, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> regained the town in 1001. It joined the Second Bulgarian Empire after the rebellion of Asen and Peter in 1186 and withstood a three-month Mongol siege in 1279. It remained Bulgarian until the Ottoman conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> around 1400.</p><p>Under Ottoman rule Silistre was the centre of a sanjak and later of a large province. It was besieged between 14 April and 23 June 1854 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, an event that inspired Namık Kemal's play Vatan Yahut Silistre. Silistra joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in 1878. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1913, was held by Bulgaria from 1916, returned to Romania by the Treaty of Neuilly in 1919 and became Bulgarian again in 1940, a transfer confirmed in 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Silistra is a cultural, industrial, transport and educational centre of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. The grave of the rabbi Eliezer Papo, who served the Jewish community from 1819 to 1826, is a place of pilgrimage. The Roman general Flavius Aëtius, who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attila">Attila the Hun</a>, is the town's best-known native.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Silistra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Mircea I (r. 1386–1418) beat the Ottomans in several battles, among them the Battle of Rovine in 1394, drove them from Dobruja and briefly held the Danube Delta and <b>Silistra</b>; he accepted peace in 1417 after Mehmed I had taken Turnu Măgurele and Giurgiu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · Military</a></li></ul></section>
